# No `Surface` for bordered cards (iOS double-border)

`react-native-paper`'s `Surface` renders its elevation in an extra layer on iOS.
When a `Surface` also has a visible border (`borderWidth` / `borderColor` /
`borderLeftWidth` / etc.), iOS draws a doubled/offset edge — most visible as a
faint second border on the right/bottom of the card.

## Rule

- **Do NOT use `Surface` for a bordered card or container.** Use a plain
  `View` instead, and draw the border yourself.
- For elevation, apply a **theme shadow token** (`shadows.sm | md | lg` from
  `useTheme()` / `import { shadows } from '@/theme'`) on the `View`. These tokens
  set `shadow*` (iOS) and `elevation` (Android) together.
- Always set an explicit `backgroundColor` on the `View` (e.g.
  `colors.background.paper`) since `Surface` previously provided the surface color.

### Bad

```tsx
<Surface
  style={[styles.card, { borderColor: colors.divider }]} // styles.card has borderWidth: 1
  elevation={1}
>
  {children}
</Surface>
```

### Good

```tsx
const { colors, shadows } = useTheme();

<View
  style={[
    styles.card, // borderWidth: 1
    shadows.sm,
    { borderColor: colors.divider, backgroundColor: colors.background.paper },
  ]}
>
  {children}
</View>
```

## When `Surface` is still fine

- A **borderless** elevated card that relies only on Paper's shadow (no
  `border*` in its resolved style) can stay a `Surface`.
- This rule is a deliberate exception to `react-native-paper.mdc` ("Paper
  first"): for **bordered** surfaces, prefer `View` for correct cross-platform
  rendering (see `cross-platform-ux.mdc`).
